/** \page page_2 Installation

\section win_install Windows Installation

\subsection win_package Installing package.

The latest package zzalog-[version].msi is available from https://sourceforge.net/projects/zzalog/. 
Once downloaded execute it and follow
instructions to install this. It asks the user whether the installation is just for
the user or all users on the machine.
If for all users ZZALOG will be installed in
"C:\Program Files\GM3ZZA\ZZALOG\bin" and the  
data used by ZZALOG will be installed in 
"C:\Program Files\GM3ZZA\ZZALOG\etc".
For a single user ZZALOG will be installed in
"C:\Users\pvros\AppData\Local\Programs\GM3ZZA\ZZALOG\bin" and the
data used in
"C:\Users\pvros\AppData\Local\Programs\GM3ZZA\ZZALOG\etc".

\subsection win_source Compiling from source

The source may be cloned from https://github.com/pvrose/zzalog. 
It will be necessary to clone the database in recursive mode.
This flow has been checked using Microsoft Visual Studio 2022 and 2026 but only with
configuration x64-Debug from scratch. 
It is recommended to run this flow within Visual Studio. Open Visual Studio and continue without
code. Open a Powershell terminal: View->Terminal then within that terminal do the following:

\par Fetch and install Hamlib.
Download the latest Hamlib release from
https://github.com/Hamlib/Hamlib.
Select the self-installing version 4.x.x Windows package. After installation follow the 
instructions in README-w64-bin.txt to recreate the .lib file (replicated here - replace 4.7.0 with the
particular version being used).
\code
cd "C:\Program Files\hamlib-w64-4.7.0\lib\mcvc"
sudo lib /def:libhamlib-4.def /machine:x64
\endcode
\note ZZALOG has not yet been tested with Hamlib version 5.
\par Fetch and build FLTK.
\code
git clone https://github.com/fltk/fltk
cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo
cmake --build build
\endcode
Alternatively, MSVC may be used to build the FLTK libraries.
\note In either case take a note of the directory used to build FLTK.
\par Installing libcurl and boost.
Fetch and install VCPKG
\code
git clone https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg.git
cd vcpkg
.\bootstrap-vcpkg.bat 
.\vcpkg.exe integrate install 
\endcode
Use VCPKG to install libcurl and boost.
\code 
.\vcpkg.exe install curl 
.\vcpkg.exe install boost
\endcode 
\par Fetch JSON and Pthread include files
\code 
cd ..
git clone https://github.com/nlohmann/json
git clone https://github.com/GetHobbelt/pthread-win32
\endcode
\par Fetch ZZALOG and the utilities package ZZACOMMON
\code
git clone --recursive https://github.com/pvrose/zzalog
\endcode

\par Building ZZALOG
To build ZZALOG open the ZZALOG folder within Visual Studio: File->Open->Folder and
select zzalog. This should now automatically run the CMake configuration step.
Select Build->Build ALL and the executable and documentation will get built. By default
the documents will not be build. To add documents, under CMake setting set the CMake 
command arguments to "-DZZALOG_DOX=ON". to further add PDF version, add "-DZZALOG_PDF=ON".

The CMake flow will default the FLTK build directory. To verride this default add
"-DFLTK_DIR=<FLTK build directory>" in the CMake command arguments as above.

\par Installing ZZALOG
To install select Build->Install ZZALOG.

\section linux_install Linux Installation

ZZALOG is available in source form. It may be cloned from 
https://github.com/pvrose/zzalog. 
It will be necessary to clone the database in recursive mode.
It has dependencies on the 
external libraries: fltk, hamlib, libcurl and nlohmann/json. The 
library pugixml is included in the source data.

The HTML userguide requires the use of Doxygen.

The PDG userguide is provided as part of the source tarball. It may be regenerated from the 
documentation source files, but requires the use of LateX.

\subsection install_deps Install dependencies

FLTK can either be compiled from source cloned from github (https://github.com/fltk/fltk) or installed form a tarball. 
It is also available in most distros. Version 1.4.3 
or greater is required.

Hamlib can either be compiled from source cloned from github (https://github.com/Hamlib/Hamlib) 
or a source tarball at https://hamlib.sourceforge.net/snapshots/. As support of new rigs
is a continual process, using versions availables from distros is not advised.

Libcurl is available in most distros.

Boost is available in most distros.

Nlohmann/json is available in most distros.

Doxygen is available in most distros.

LateX is available in most distros. For debian (and raspbian) it requires texlive-latex-basic
and texlive-latex-extra.

\subsection install_proc Installation

Clone the source from github (https://github.com/pvrose/zzalog) including submodules.

\code
git clone --recursive https://github.com/pvrose/zzalog
\endcode

The build and install processes use CMake.

The following CMake flag is available:
\par ZZALOG_DOX
Default: <b>OFF</b>. Generates HTML versions of Userguide and API Documenation and the PDF
version of the Userguide.
\par ZZALOG_PDF
Default: <b>OFF</b>, Generates PDF version of Userguide (requires ZZALOG_DOX=ON)

To build ZZALOG with documentation (HTML & PDF):

\code
cd [zzalog]\n
cmake -B build -DZZALOG_DOX=ON -DZZALOG_PDF=ON\n
cmake --build build\n
\endcode

and without:

\code
cd [zzalog]\n
cmake -B build\n
cmake --build build\n
\endcode

These create the executable <i>build/zzalog</i>.

To install ZZALOG:

\code
cd [zzalog]\n
cmake --install build
\endcode

This copies the executable <i>zzalog</i> to /usr/local/bin and the reference data to 
/usr/local/etc/zzalog.
The CMake switch CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX may be applied to create the executable and reference data
in user-specific filestore. For example:

\code
cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=~/.local
cmake --build build
cmake --install build
\endcode

...will install the executable <i>~/.local/bin/zzalog</i> and the reference data in <i>~/.local/etc/zzalog</i>.
\section initial_run First-time Run (Cold-start)

The first time ZZALOG is run, a dialog opens asking for station details. 
The selection can be either for a club station or an individual station.
Select one or the other, then fill in the form. 
\note For individual stations, callsign, operator name and location identifier
must be entered. For club stations, callsign, club name and location identifier
must be entered.

\image html init_1.png "New installaton"
\image latex init_1.png "New installaton" width=8cm

- <B>Club</B> Select this for a club installation.
- <B>Individual</B> Select this for a personal installation.
- <B>Callsign</B> Enter the default station callsign to be used. This will be
used to generate the default filename for the logbook.
- <B>Club name</B> Enter the club's name (not available for individual).
- <B>Location</B> Enter an identifier to know the main station address by.
- <B>Gridsquare</B> Enter the Maidenhead gridsquare locator - 
used for initially locating the station.
- <B>Name</B> Enter the name of the usual operator (not available for a club).

\image html init_2.png "Individual details"
\image latex init_2.png "Individual details" width=8cm

Select "Accept" to proceed.

The other tabs can then be used to provide further information on the station.

\image html init_4.png "Specifying location details"
\image latex init_4.png "Specifying location details" width=8cm
\image html init_5.png "Specifying operator details"
\image latex init_5.png "Specifying operator details" width=8cm
\image html init_6.png "Specifying callsign details"
\image latex init_6.png "Specifying callsign details" width=8cm

Close this window to proceed.

Dialogs then open to select:
- a directory to store QSL card images.
- a file to store the logbook. The name of the file will default to the 
staion callsign.

\subsection warm_start Warm-start

A warm start is where a logbook file already exists. ZZALOG will use information in that 
logbook to provide more information about locations, operators and station callsigns
already present.

\image html warm_1.png "Adding location data from logbook"
\image latex warm_1.png "Adding location data from logbook" width=10cm

In the above image the status log shows that data from the logbook has been added to the
station data. This is shown in the dialog below:

\image html warm_2.png "More details about location"
\image latex warm_2.png "More details about location" width=10cm

It also shows that another location has been used. In this case a different Maidenhead
locator is used. The location "Unknown 1" has been created to represent it. This
can be renamed as in the image below.

\image html warm_3.png "Renaming an unknown location"
\image latex warm_3.png "Renaming an unknown location" width=10cm

It can also be modified.

\image html warm_4.png "Modified location"
\image latex warm_4.png "Modified location" width=10cm


*/